  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...While wind and seas have come down, minor \nsoundside flooding (1 ft or less) is still possible for mainly\nDowneast Carteret county through the next several high tide\ncycles. The high astronomical tides peak on Tuesday, and already\nhigh water levels due to last weeks wind event will keep water\nlevels elevated mainly around the high tide cycles through the\nnext couple days. Standing water on roadways, and in low lying \nor poor drainage areas, could have minor impacts for travellers\nin especially vulnerable areas prone to the tidal flooding. \nElevated water levels will persist through Wednesday, but \nlevels are not expected to be as high as they were the last\nseveral days.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cool nights and warm days are the theme over the\nnext several days as a dry air mass persists overhead. Mid to\nlate week, upper level ridging is forecast to expand north and \neast from the Gulf Coast to the Southeast U.S. coast. Warming \nlow- level thicknesses and the eventual return deep layer sswrly\nflow will allow temps to soar above normal to close out \nSeptember and welcome in October. Moisture and humidity will \ngradually return as well, but it doesn't look like a quick \nreturn. Overnight temps will cool to the low side of guidance,\nand have trended lower due to the dry air mass in place and\nlight to calm wind each night. Areas in the swrn zones, as well\nas adjacent to the normal cold spots such as near the Croatan\nand Hoffman forests will be the coolest with mid-upper 50s\nexpected tonight, then upper 50s to low 60s Tue night. High\ntemps will begin to rise above climo tomorrow with mid 80s\ninterior (upr 70s coast) then upper 80s to near 90 Wed-Thu (upr\n70s/low 80s coast), with some low 90s to round out the work\nweek. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Model differences on timing of the next system,\nbut it appears a cold front and GOM moisture begin to impinge on \nthe region either this weekend or early next week with the next \nchance of rain. For now, pops have been raised to no higher than\n30-50% for this period of time.",
